'''Potocsí''' ({{IPA|ca|poˈtoksi|pron}}), officially the '''Republic of Potocsí''' ({{lang-ca|República de Potocsí|links=yes}}), is a country located on the western coast of [[Abyala]]. It is bordered by the [[Salsipuedes Sea]] and [[Galibian Sea]] to the north, [[Balisca]] to the south and east, [[Asikyira]] to the west, and [[Galibi]] to the southwest. With an area of 916,445 km² (353,841 sq mi) and a population of approximately 72.6 million, Potocsí is the [nth]-largest country in Abyala by both area and population. The capital and largest city is [[Port Soledat]], located on the northern coast. Other major cities include [[Ciudad Obrera]], [[San Lorenzo]], [[Port Ric]], and [[Valverde]]. Potocsí is a [[unitary state|unitary]] [[presidential system|presidential]] [[republic]], though it experienced a prolonged period of [[military dictatorship]] from 1971 to 1985.

Potocsí's territory encompasses a diverse range of environments, from the [[Matarrañya]] highlands in the southeast to the [[Llanos]] grasslands in the central region and the [[Salsipuedes Sea|Salsipuedes]] coastal lowlands in the north. The country possesses significant [[W:Petroleum|petroleum]] reserves, primarily located in the [[Marañón basin]] region disputed with Balisca. This territorial dispute has been a defining feature of Potocsí's foreign relations and domestic politics throughout the 20th century.

The territory of modern Potocsí was colonized by [[Volisania]] in the 16th century, achieving independence in 1811 as part of the broader [[Abyalan Wars of Independence]]. The post-independence period was marked by [[political instability]], [[W:Caudillo|caudillisme]], and intermittent [[civil war]]s. The discovery of oil in the early 20th century transformed the economy but also exacerbated regional tensions and inequality. The late 20th century saw significant political turmoil, including the [[Potocsino military junta|military junta period]] (1971–1985), the [[Camboriú War]] with Balisca (1976–1977), and the subsequent [[Potocsino democratic transition|democratic transition]]. Since 1985, Potocsí has maintained a democratic system, though it continues to face challenges including [[W:Corruption|corruption]], [[W:Economic inequality|economic inequality]], and the unresolved [[Marañón dispute|Marañón basin dispute]].

==Etymology==
It is believed that ''Potosí'' is a "[[Kawsani language|Kawsani]]" word. However, in Kawsani the root ''p'otoj'' does not refer to a thunderous noise, whereas it does in "[[Jachani language|Jachani]]". Thus, if ''Potosí'' encompasses the idea of a thunderous noise, the location would have a Jachani root rather than a Kawsani.

The actual sharp structure of the term is contrary to the nature of both Jachani and Kawsani. Another explanation, given by several Kawsani speakers, is that ''potoq'' is an [[W:Onomatopoeic|onomatopoeic]] word that reproduces the sound of the hammer against the ore, and oral tradition has it that the town derived its name from this word.

