===Foreign relations===
{{Main|Foreign relations of Potocsí}}

Potocsí maintains diplomatic relations with most countries and is a member of the [[Adonian Community]], the [[Abyalan Union]], the [[Northern Cone Economic Partnership]], and the [[Non-Aligned Movement]].

The most significant foreign policy issue is the ongoing [[Balisca-Potocsí conflict]] over the [[Marañón basin]]. Despite the [[Kuluba Accords]] (1982) and the restoration of diplomatic relations in 1984, the sovereignty dispute remains unresolved.

Potocsí has historically maintained close relations with [[Breisland]] and [[Volisania]], which have been major investors in the country's oil industry. During the [[military junta]] period, both countries provided military and economic support. Relations with [[Burawa]] and the [[Socialist Bloc]] were strained during the Cold War but have improved since the democratic transition.

===Military===
{{Main|Potocsino Armed Forces}}

The [[Potocsino Armed Forces]] ({{lang|ca|Forces Armades de Potocsí}}) consist of four branches:

* '''[[Potocsino Army]]''' ({{lang|ca|Exèrcit de Potocsí}}) — 185,000 active personnel
* '''[[Potocsino Navy]]''' ({{lang|ca|Armada de Potocsí}}) — 45,000 active personnel
* '''[[Potocsino Air Force]]''' ({{lang|ca|Força Aèria de Potocsí}}) — 25,000 active personnel
* '''[[Potocsino National Guard]]''' ({{lang|ca|Guàrdia Nacional de Potocsí}}) — 35,000 active personnel

Total active personnel: approximately 290,000, with an additional 500,000 in reserve.

==Economy==
{{Main|Economy of Potocsí}}

Potocsí has a [[mixed economy]] dominated by the [[petroleum]] sector, which accounts for approximately 30% of [[GDP]], 90% of [[export]] earnings, and 50% of government revenues. The country is one of Abyala's leading oil exporters and a founding member of the [[Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries (Abyala)|OPEC-A]].

===Petroleum industry===

{{Main|Petroleum industry in Potocsí}}

The petroleum industry is the backbone of the Potocsino economy. The country possesses the [nth]-largest proven oil reserves in the world, estimated at 85 billion barrels, primarily located in the [[Marañón basin]].

The state-owned [[Potocsino Petroleum]] (PETROPOTO) dominates the industry, though foreign companies also operate in the country. Major oil fields include [[Kuluba]], [[Yurimaguas]], [[Iquitos]], and [[Maracaibo]].

The industry has been a source of both wealth and conflict. The [[Balisca-Potocsí conflict]] centers on control of the Marañón basin's oil resources, and the [[Marañón oil fires]] of 1976–1977 caused an estimated $4.5 billion in damage to Potocsino oil infrastructure.

===Other sectors===

* '''Manufacturing:''' Includes [[petrochemical]]s, [[steel]], [[aluminum]], [[automotive assembly]], and [[food processing]].
* '''Agriculture:''' Major products include [[coffee]], [[cacao]], [[sugar]], [[rice]], [[corn]], and [[cattle]].
* '''Mining:''' Potocsí has significant deposits of [[iron ore]], [[gold]], [[bauxite]], and [[coal]].
* '''Tourism:''' An growing sector, focused on [[natural attractions]] such as [[Angel Falls]] and the [[Matarrañya Highlands]].

==Demographics==
{{Main|Demographics of Potocsí}}

The population of Potocsí is estimated at 35.7 million (2023), making it the [nth]-most populous country in Abyala. The population is concentrated in the northern coastal region, particularly around [[Puerto Soledad]] and the [[Maracaibo]] area.

