==Culture==
{{Main|Culture of Potocsí}}

Potocsino culture reflects a blend of [[indigenous]], [[Volisanian]], [[Ethnic groups of Kaftia|Kaftian]], and other influences. The country is known for its [[music]], [[literature]], [[cuisine]], and [[festivals]].

===Music===

Potocsino music encompasses diverse genres, including [[joropo]], [[salsa]], [[merengue]], and [[gaita]]. The [[maraca]] and [[cuatro]] are iconic instruments. Notable musicians include [[Simón Díaz]], [[Ali Primera]], and [[Los Melódicos]].

===Literature===

Potocsino literature has produced notable writers including [[Rómulo Gallegos]], [[Arturo Uslar Pietri]], and [[Andrés Eloy Blanco]]. The [[Latin American Boom|Abyalan Boom]] included Potocsino writers such as [[Salvador Garmendia]]. Literature is primarily written in [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], though some indigenous-language literature has gained recognition.

===Cuisine===

Potocsino cuisine varies by region but typically includes [[arepa]]s ({{lang|ca|arepes}}), [[pabellón criollo]] ({{lang|ca|pabelló crioll}}), [[hallaca]]s, [[tequeño]]s, and [[asado]]. The country is also known for its [[cacao]] and [[rum]].

===Sports===

[[Baseball]] ({{lang|ca|beisbol}}) is the most popular sport in Potocsí, with a professional league established in 1945. The national team has won several [[Abyalan Baseball Championship]]s. [[Football (soccer)|Football]] ({{lang|ca|futbol}}), [[basketball]], and [[boxing]] are also popular.

